<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Han Chinese</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Han Chinese are the majority ethnic group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, taking their name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>. They were the core population of native dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> they were ruled by Mongol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> elites, a relationship that shaped governance and, in the Qing case, nationalist opposition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/han-chinese.jpg" alt="Han Chinese" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%AD%A6%E5%AE%98%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Han Chinese trace their ancestry and culture to the Huaxia, a confederation of agricultural tribes of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> that lived along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> basins in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Early Chinese history is largely mythical: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Records of the Grand Historian records successive overlords, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-sovereigns-and-five-emperors">Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors</a>, for whom little reliable archaeological evidence exists. The name Han people first appeared during the Northern and Southern period and was inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, and the Chinese language and script came to be called the Han language and Han characters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and Han rule sent settlers south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> is said to have sent several hundred thousand men and fifteen thousand women to settle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingnan">Lingnan</a> under a general named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a>, and the Han emperor Wu later ordered a further two hundred thousand men to build ships to attack and colonize the region. Successive migrations southward fused the newcomers with the peoples south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, while the centres of Han wealth and culture moved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> basin to Jiangnan. By the Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties the Han were the main inhabitants of the fertile lowlands and cities of the south.</p><p>Han Chinese have often held the dominant positions of governance, but dynastic rule was in notable periods held by others, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> (916–1125), the Jin (1115–1234), the Yuan (1271–1368) and the Qing (1644–1912). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> made the restoration of Han rule a tenet of the 1911 Revolution, which overthrew the Manchu-led Qing and established the Han-dominated Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Scholars such as Kevin Carrico and Dru Gladney argue that a single Han identity, set against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> rulers, is a modern development promoted by nationalists near the end of the Qing. Liang Qichao coined the term Zhonghua minzu between 1902 and 1905 to refer to the Han; it later became a wider concept for all the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Han Chinese make up about 91 percent of the population of China. Their literary heritage includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Tao Te Ching</a> and the Four Great Classical Novels.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Han Chinese” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jurchen were a minority in an empire whose census of 1207 counted more than 45 million people, most of them <b>Han Chinese</b> farmers of the North China Plain, alongside Khitan, Bohai and other peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Manchuria, the homeland of the Manchu people and by the 20th century overwhelmingly <b>Han Chinese</b>, had been contested since Russia annexed Outer Manchuria by the unequal treaties of 1858 and 1860.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The early dynasty relied on disciplined military organisation and administrative restructuring, and claimed legitimacy by reasserting <b>Han Chinese</b> traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing military rested on the Eight Banners of Manchu, Mongol and Han bannermen that had conquered China, supplemented by the Green Standard Army, a much larger force of <b>Han Chinese</b> soldiers dispersed in smaller garrisons across the provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tanguts, <b>Han Chinese</b>, Tibetans and Uyghurs served as officials and intermarried freely, and Tangut, Chinese and Tibetan were all official languages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese folk religion is the traditional religion of the <b>Han Chinese</b>: the worship of gods, immortals and ancestors, bound to villages, lineages and temples rather than to a church or creed.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
